    "summary": "Mellow ’72 set with soaring Bird Song and expansive Truckin’ > The Other One",
    "blurb": "The performance shines on a soaring Bird Song and an expansive Truckin' > The Other One sequence, while the first set delivers solid renditions of Big River and China Cat Sunflower. Jerry’s clean guitar tone and Phil’s tight bass underpin a balanced show that moves from medium‑energy grooves to high‑octane moments.",
    "review": "September 30, 1972 at Reeves Field finds the Dead in a relaxed yet deeply musical stride. The first set rolls through classic Americana numbers before launching into a luminous Bird Song that lets Jerry’s crisp guitar and vocal phrasing breathe, and a bright China Cat Sunflower that, despite being slightly buried in some mixes, still glitters with its signature lift into I Know You Rider. The second set eases into a mellow groove before exploding on Truckin' > The Other One, where the band stretches out for an extended jam that showcases Phil’s “ON!” bass foundation and Bob’s solid rhythm chops, while Keith’s piano moments linger just beneath the surface. Closing with Sugar Magnolia, Mississippi Half‑Step Uptown Toodeloo and a spirited encore of One More Saturday Night, the show balances exploratory improvisation with tight ensemble playing, making it a standout entry in the early‑’70s catalog.",
